/// Whether two erased values are equal: what `==` answers when both of its
/// operands are a `dyn Trait` or a Host `Any`, whose concrete type is known only
/// once the box is opened.
///
/// Nothing in a program calls this. The lowering does, at every `==`, `!=` and
/// `assertEqual` whose two operands are boxed, and from inside a walk it
/// composed for a known layout wherever that walk reaches a boxed field,
/// element or payload. Where the layout is known it never gets here: a
/// comparison of two `Point`s is a function the lowering writes for `Point`
/// (ADR 0064's Decision 3), and this is the one fallback for what no layout
/// describes (ADR 0068's Decisions 4 and 5).
///
/// # What it answers
///
/// Two values are equal when they have **one semantic type** and then **one
/// structure**, compared part for part in the order `core.dynamicChild` gives:
///
/// - **The type first.** `core.dynamicSameType` holds: the same kind and, for a
///   struct, an enum and a range, the same declared name. Type arguments are not
///   part of the name — an `Option<Int>` and an `Option<String>` are one type, and
///   so are a `m.Cell<Int>` and a `m.Cell<String>` — which is what makes `None`
///   equal to `None` whichever `Option` it came out of. Two values of different
///   types are unequal and never an error: an `Int` is not a `Duration` however
///   many nanoseconds it is, an `Array` is not a `Vector` holding the same
///   elements, and an `Error` is not the `String` its message is.
/// - **`()`** is equal to `()`.
/// - **`Bool`, `Int` and `Duration`** are equal when they are the same value.
/// - **`Float`** is IEEE 754's `==`: a `NaN` is equal to nothing, itself
///   included, and `0.0` is equal to `-0.0`.
/// - **`String`** is equal when the bytes are.
/// - **A function, and every opaque value** — a Host handle, a task, a task
///   scope, a `Shared` cell, a byte run — is equal to **nothing, itself
///   included**. The language gives none of them an equality, and a box does not
///   give them one (ADR 0068's Decision 7).
/// - **An enum** is equal when the case is the same and then the case's payload
///   is, part for part in declaration order.
/// - **A struct and a range** are equal when their fields are, in declaration
///   order. An `opaque` struct is a struct here: its fields are private to the
///   module that declared it, and to this module, which only compares them.
/// - **An `Array`, a `Vector`, a `Set` and a `Map`** are equal when they hold the
///   same number of children and the children are equal in order. A `Set` is in
///   its one ascending order and a `Map` is its entries in that order, key then
///   value, so two of either with the same members line up.
///
/// It answers `false` at the first difference and reads nothing after it.
///
/// # A value that contains itself is refused
///
/// A `Vector` is the one value these walks follow that a program can change
/// after building it, so it is the one that can hold itself: push a struct into
/// a vector inside that struct, and the value has no end. Comparing it is
/// **refused**, on the line of the `==` — not answered `false`, which would be a
/// claim about two values nobody finished comparing, and not left to run until
/// fuel or a deadline stops it, which would make a limit part of what `==` means
/// (issue #493).
///
/// What is refused is a cycle **on the path being walked**, and nothing else.
/// The walk keeps the pairs of vectors it is inside — one from each side,
/// descended into and not yet finished — and a pair it meets again while still
/// inside it is the refusal. A pair is left when everything under it has been
/// compared, so a vector reached twice by two routes, which is a part two
/// fields share and not a cycle, is compared twice like any other part. Whether
/// two views are one vector is `core.dynamicSameObject`, the one identity
/// question reflection answers; it is asked here and nowhere a program can see.
///
/// The refusal comes at the first pair met again in the order the walk visits,
/// so a difference visited before it is still `false`. That order is the one
/// the oracle's `==` and the walks composed for a known layout visit in, and
/// the three refuse in the same words: `Value::eq_value` and `lower::synth`
/// raise `cove_ir::dynamic::CONTAINS_ITSELF` and the two sentences beside it,
/// and [`containsItself`] and the two beside it here are those sentences.
///
/// # There is no nesting bound
///
/// The walk is a loop over two explicit stacks of views, one per side, and
/// never a recursion: a value nested ten thousand deep is compared exactly as
/// one nested ten deep is, on either evaluator, and neither's call depth is a
/// limit the language has (issue #480's decision). What bounds it is the work
/// it does, which is charged an instruction at a time like any other loop. The
/// path of vectors above is not a bound either: a finite value, however deep,
/// never meets a pair it is inside.
///
/// Children are pushed last first so that they are popped first first: the
/// visit is depth first and left to right, which is the order `a == b` has
/// always meant, so the first difference found is the leftmost.
///
/// # A value one level deep allocates nothing
///
/// Both views are opened and compared before a stack exists, and a pair of
/// scalars, strings, functions, opaque values, empty collections or cases
/// without a payload is answered there: comparing two boxed `Int`s is two
/// opens, a type test and one comparison. The children of the two are then
/// compared in order where they are, and the two stacks are made only at the
/// first child that has children of its own — so a struct of scalars, an
/// `Array<Int>`, a `Vector<Int>` or a `Some(3)` is compared without allocating,
/// and a value that really nests pays for its stacks once, however deep it goes.
///
/// The path is made later still: only once a vector with children is descended
/// into, by [`tracked`]. A value that nests without a vector — structs, enums,
/// arrays, maps — never pays for it.
fn equals(a: Any, b: Any) -> Bool {
  let left = core.dynamicOpen(a)
  let right = core.dynamicOpen(b)
  let count = children(left, right)
  var at = 0
  while at < count {
    let under = children(
      core.dynamicChild(left, at),
      core.dynamicChild(right, at),
    )
    if under < 0 {
      return false
    }
    if under > 0 {
      return deeper(left, right, count, at, under)
    }
    at = at + 1
  }
  // A `count` of `-1` ran no turn: the two roots were not alike.
  count >= 0
}

/// The rest of [`equals`] from child `from` of `left` and `right` on — a pair
/// that is alike and has `under` children of its own each — over the two
/// stacks.
///
/// Every child before `from` has been compared and had nothing under it, so
/// what is left to visit is `from`'s children and then its later siblings,
/// `count` in all. They are pushed siblings first and each run last first, so
/// that `from`'s first child is on top.
///
/// This loop keeps no path, and it does not need one until it descends into a
/// vector: the first vector with children it meets — the root, `from`, or any
/// pair it pops — hands the walk to [`tracked`], which carries it on from
/// there with the path made.
fn deeper(
  left: DynamicView,
  right: DynamicView,
  count: Int,
  from: Int,
  under: Int,
) -> Bool {
  let lefts: Vector<DynamicView> = core.vectorWithCapacity(count)
  let rights: Vector<DynamicView> = core.vectorWithCapacity(count)
  var at = count - 1
  while at > from {
    stack(lefts, core.dynamicChild(left, at))
    stack(rights, core.dynamicChild(right, at))
    at = at - 1
  }
  let x = core.dynamicChild(left, from)
  let y = core.dynamicChild(right, from)
  // The two stacks are always the same height, so one count serves both.
  var depth = count - 1 - from
  // Two `if`s rather than one `||`, for `std.string.sliceBytes`' reason.
  if core.dynamicKind(left) == 9 {
    let way: Vector<DynamicView> = core.vectorWithCapacity(8)
    let ends: Vector<Int> = core.vectorWithCapacity(4)
    // The root's children are everything the stacks will ever hold, so it is
    // left only when the walk is over.
    stack(way, left)
    stack(way, right)
    mark(ends, 0)
    return tracked(lefts, rights, way, ends, depth, x, y, under)
  }
  if core.dynamicKind(x) == 9 {
    let way: Vector<DynamicView> = core.vectorWithCapacity(8)
    let ends: Vector<Int> = core.vectorWithCapacity(4)
    return tracked(lefts, rights, way, ends, depth, x, y, under)
  }
  var next = under - 1
  while next >= 0 {
    stack(lefts, core.dynamicChild(x, next))
    stack(rights, core.dynamicChild(y, next))
    next = next - 1
  }
  depth = depth + under
  while depth > 0 {
    depth = depth - 1
    let x = core.vectorLoad(lefts, depth)
    let y = core.vectorLoad(rights, depth)
    core.vectorTruncate(lefts, depth)
    core.vectorTruncate(rights, depth)
    var next = children(x, y) - 1
    if next < -1 {
      return false
    }
    if next >= 0 {
      if core.dynamicKind(x) == 9 {
        let way: Vector<DynamicView> = core.vectorWithCapacity(8)
        let ends: Vector<Int> = core.vectorWithCapacity(4)
        return tracked(lefts, rights, way, ends, depth, x, y, next + 1)
      }
      while next >= 0 {
        stack(lefts, core.dynamicChild(x, next))
        stack(rights, core.dynamicChild(y, next))
        depth = depth + 1
        next = next - 1
      }
    }
  }
  true
}

/// The rest of [`deeper`]'s walk once it has a vector to be inside: `x` and
/// `y` — alike, with `under` children each — expanded above the `depth` pairs
/// on `lefts` and `rights`, and then those, with the path of vector pairs kept
/// in `way` and `ends`.
///
/// `way` holds the pairs of vectors the walk is inside, outermost first, as a
/// left view and then a right one; `ends` holds, for each, how high the stacks
/// were when its children went on — so its children are the pairs at and above
/// that height, and it is finished once the walk pops a pair below it. The
/// visit is depth first, so the pairs that are finished are always the
/// innermost, and leaving them is truncating both.
///
/// A vector pair with children is looked for on the path before it is added
/// to it, and finding it is the refusal. A vector pair without children is
/// never looked for: nothing can be under it, so it cannot close a cycle, and
/// a vector on the path is never empty.
fn tracked(
  lefts: Vector<DynamicView>,
  rights: Vector<DynamicView>,
  way: Vector<DynamicView>,
  ends: Vector<Int>,
  depth: Int,
  x: DynamicView,
  y: DynamicView,
  under: Int,
) -> Bool {
  var depth = depth
  // The innermost pair's end, kept beside `ends` so that the question every
  // pair popped asks — is the innermost pair finished — is one comparison. A
  // path that holds only the root, or nothing, never finishes: `-1`, or the
  // root's `0`, is never above a height.
  var edge = -1
  let held = core.vectorLength(ends)
  if held > 0 {
    edge = core.vectorLoad(ends, held - 1)
  }
  if core.dynamicKind(x) == 9 {
    if onPath(way, x, y) {
      core.refuse(containsItself(), containsItselfRule(), containsItselfHelp())
    }
    stack(way, x)
    stack(way, y)
    mark(ends, depth)
    edge = depth
  }
  var next = under - 1
  while next >= 0 {
    stack(lefts, core.dynamicChild(x, next))
    stack(rights, core.dynamicChild(y, next))
    depth = depth + 1
    next = next - 1
  }
  while depth > 0 {
    depth = depth - 1
    let x = core.vectorLoad(lefts, depth)
    let y = core.vectorLoad(rights, depth)
    core.vectorTruncate(lefts, depth)
    core.vectorTruncate(rights, depth)
    // Every pair whose children all sat above this one is finished: the
    // innermost first, and then as many outward as ended above it too. A pair
    // is left once, so the loop turns once a pair over the whole walk.
    if edge > depth {
      var inside = core.vectorLength(ends) - 1
      edge = -1
      while inside > 0 {
        let end = core.vectorLoad(ends, inside - 1)
        if end <= depth {
          edge = end
          break
        }
        inside = inside - 1
      }
      core.vectorTruncate(ends, inside)
      core.vectorTruncate(way, 2 * inside)
    }
    var next = children(x, y) - 1
    if next < -1 {
      return false
    }
    if next >= 0 {
      if core.dynamicKind(x) == 9 {
        if onPath(way, x, y) {
          core.refuse(
            containsItself(),
            containsItselfRule(),
            containsItselfHelp(),
          )
        }
        stack(way, x)
        stack(way, y)
        mark(ends, depth)
        edge = depth
      }
      while next >= 0 {
        stack(lefts, core.dynamicChild(x, next))
        stack(rights, core.dynamicChild(y, next))
        depth = depth + 1
        next = next - 1
      }
    }
  }
  true
}

/// Whether the pair of vectors `x` and `y` is on the path `way`: one entry at
/// which the left is `x` and the right is `y`.
///
/// The pair and not either side alone. `a == a` compares a value with itself
/// all the way down, so every left is its right, and it is only a cycle when
/// the *same* two are met again; and two distinct values that each contain
/// themselves are a cycle as soon as their two vectors come round together,
/// whatever else either side holds.
fn onPath(way: Vector<DynamicView>, x: DynamicView, y: DynamicView) -> Bool {
  var at = core.vectorLength(way)
  while at > 0 {
    at = at - 2
    if core.dynamicSameObject(core.vectorLoad(way, at), x) {
      if core.dynamicSameObject(core.vectorLoad(way, at + 1), y) {
        return true
      }
    }
  }
  false
}

/// What `==` says of a value that contains itself: `cove_ir::dynamic`'s
/// `CONTAINS_ITSELF`, word for word, which the oracle and the walks composed
/// for a known layout raise.
fn containsItself() -> String {
  "this value contains itself, so `==` cannot compare it"
}

/// The rule [`containsItself`] is refused under.
fn containsItselfRule() -> String {
  "Equality compares finite structure, and a value that reaches itself through a `Vector` has no end to compare."
}

/// What [`containsItself`] suggests instead.
fn containsItselfHelp() -> String {
  "compare the parts that do not lead back to the value, or use `is` to ask whether two vectors are the same one"
}

/// How many children `x` and `y` each have if they agree in everything but
/// their children — the same type, the same scalar, the same case, the same
/// number of children to compare next — and `-1` if they do not.
///
/// One answer rather than a `Bool` and a count asked for separately, because
/// every caller wants both and the count is one more observation a node.
///
/// The kind codes are `cove_ir::DynamicKind`'s one table: 0 unit, 1 bool, 2
/// int, 3 float, 4 duration, 5 string, 6 struct, 7 enum, 8 array, 9 vector,
/// 10 set, 11 map, 12 range, 13 function and 14 opaque. The same type is the
/// same kind, so one kind is asked for. A scalar has no children, and neither
/// has a function or an opaque value, which is equal to nothing.
///
/// The counts are compared even where the type settles them — two structs of
/// one declaration have one field count, two cases of one enum one payload —
/// because a count is what a collection is compared by first, and one
/// comparison for every kind is shorter than a list of the kinds that need it.
fn children(x: DynamicView, y: DynamicView) -> Int {
  if !core.dynamicSameType(x, y) {
    return -1
  }
  let kind = core.dynamicKind(x)
  if kind == 2 {
    if core.dynamicInt(x) == core.dynamicInt(y) {
      return 0
    }
    return -1
  }
  if kind == 5 {
    if core.dynamicString(x) == core.dynamicString(y) {
      return 0
    }
    return -1
  }
  if kind == 1 {
    if core.dynamicBool(x) == core.dynamicBool(y) {
      return 0
    }
    return -1
  }
  if kind == 3 {
    if core.dynamicFloat(x) == core.dynamicFloat(y) {
      return 0
    }
    return -1
  }
  if kind == 4 {
    if core.dynamicDuration(x) == core.dynamicDuration(y) {
      return 0
    }
    return -1
  }
  if kind >= 13 {
    return -1
  }
  if kind == 7 {
    if core.dynamicCase(x) != core.dynamicCase(y) {
      return -1
    }
  }
  let count = core.dynamicChildCount(x)
  if count == core.dynamicChildCount(y) {
    return count
  }
  -1
}

/// Pushes `view` onto `views`: ADR 0062's ensure, store and commit, written out
/// rather than called as `std.vector.push`, which is the same four statements.
fn stack(views: Vector<DynamicView>, view: DynamicView) {
  let at = core.vectorLength(views)
  core.vectorEnsure(views, 1)
  core.vectorStore(views, at, view)
  core.vectorCommit(views, 1)
}

/// Pushes `end` onto `ends`: [`stack`] for the heights at which [`tracked`]'s
/// pairs of vectors are finished.
fn mark(ends: Vector<Int>, end: Int) {
  let at = core.vectorLength(ends)
  core.vectorEnsure(ends, 1)
  core.vectorStore(ends, at, end)
  core.vectorCommit(ends, 1)
}
